      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Petitioners are former students of Georgia Gwinnett College who wished to exercise their religion by sharing their faith on campus while enrolled there."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Eleventh Circuit",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "370f9dc1eff8638da88f024d82e2dcd6dbcf49c91eaabcfdfb77ab6bbb14b3b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-370f9dc1eff8-0000-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Eleventh Circuit held that while a request for nominal damages can sometimes save a case from mootness, such as where a person pleads but fails to prove an amount of compensatory damages, the students' plea for nominal damages alone could not by itself establish standing."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "370f9dc1eff8638da88f024d82e2dcd6dbcf49c91eaabcfdfb77ab6bbb14b3b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-370f9dc1eff8-0000-0003",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "asks whether an award of nominal damages by itself can redress a past injury. We hold that it can."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "370f9dc1eff8638da88f024d82e2dcd6dbcf49c91eaabcfdfb77ab6bbb14b3b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-370f9dc1eff8-0001-0001",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Because nominal damages are in fact damages paid to the plaintiff, they \u201caffec[t] the behavior of the defendant towards the plaintiff \u201d and thus independently provide redress."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "370f9dc1eff8638da88f024d82e2dcd6dbcf49c91eaabcfdfb77ab6bbb14b3b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-370f9dc1eff8-0002-0000",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Lord Holt was alone in dissent in Ashby (no shame there), and although his opinion has been cited favorably by subsequent cases and commentary, his colleagues disagreed with him."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "370f9dc1eff8638da88f024d82e2dcd6dbcf49c91eaabcfdfb77ab6bbb14b3b8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-370f9dc1eff8-0002-0001",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Court acknowledges that \u201cthe rule allowing nominal damages for a violation of any legal right . . . was not universally followed,\u201d ante, at 288, but even this concession understates the equivocal nature of the historical record."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
